@baseUrl = https://localhost:7217

# ConferenceProFlow — M2.6E: full round-trip allocation demo (local development only).
# Prerequisites: SQL Server, RabbitMQ, Registration API and Allocation Worker are running.
# The 5 required background services are enabled via local User Secrets.
# Run each request in order in Visual Studio using "Send Request".
# Requests automatically reuse IDs from earlier JSON responses; do NOT enter GUIDs into HTTP requests.
# Participant emails contain a fresh generated GUID, allowing repeatable demos.
# IMPORTANT: STOP after request 2. Seed capacity=2 in the Allocation database using
# docs/ALLOCATION_E2E_SEED.sql; paste ONLY the new session ID into that SQL file.
# After the seed succeeds, run requests 3-8; allow async consumers/publishers time to work.
# Finally run GET requests 9-11. Expect TWO Confirmed and ONE Rejected in total.
# Do not assume which particular participant gets Rejected; event order can differ.

### 1. Create a dedicated conference
# @name demoCreateConference
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/conferences
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "ConferenceProFlow Allocation Roundtrip Demo 2027",
  "startsAtUtc": "2027-06-01T08:00:00Z",
  "endsAtUtc": "2027-06-02T18:00:00Z"
}

### 2. Create a new session (copy this response's 'id' ONCE into the SQL seed)
# @name demoCreateSession
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/conferences/{{demoCreateConference.response.body.$.id}}/sessions
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Reliable Messaging and Capacity-Safe Allocation",
  "startsAtUtc": "2027-06-01T10:00:00Z",
  "endsAtUtc": "2027-06-01T11:30:00Z"
}

### 3. Create Nora Ellis (unique test email on every run)
# STOP — run docs/ALLOCATION_E2E_SEED.sql in the LOCAL ConferenceProFlowAllocation database.
# Replace PASTE-SESSION-GUID-HERE with the 'id' from request 2. Check Capacity=2, ReservedCount=0.
# Only then proceed to the three registrations.
# @name demoCreateParticipant1
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/participants
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "displayName": "Nora Ellis",
  "email": "nora.ellis.m26e.{{$guid}}@example.com"
}

### 4. Register Nora for the new session
# @name demoCreateRegistration1
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/registrations
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "participantId": "{{demoCreateParticipant1.response.body.$.id}}",
  "sessionId": "{{demoCreateSession.response.body.$.id}}"
}

### 5. Create Ethan Brooks (unique test email on every run)
# @name demoCreateParticipant2
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/participants
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "displayName": "Ethan Brooks",
  "email": "ethan.brooks.m26e.{{$guid}}@example.com"
}

### 6. Register Ethan for the same session
# @name demoCreateRegistration2
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/registrations
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "participantId": "{{demoCreateParticipant2.response.body.$.id}}",
  "sessionId": "{{demoCreateSession.response.body.$.id}}"
}

### 7. Create Maya Chen (unique test email on every run)
# @name demoCreateParticipant3
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/participants
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "displayName": "Maya Chen",
  "email": "maya.chen.m26e.{{$guid}}@example.com"
}

### 8. Register Maya for the same session
# @name demoCreateRegistration3
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/registrations
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "participantId": "{{demoCreateParticipant3.response.body.$.id}}",
  "sessionId": "{{demoCreateSession.response.body.$.id}}"
}

### 9. Verify Nora's Registration status
# Wait briefly for async message handling, then run requests 9-11.
# The three final Registration statuses should contain TWO Confirmed and ONE Rejected.
# If any still show Pending, check the background-service logs, RabbitMQ queues and Outbox tables.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/registrations/{{demoCreateRegistration1.response.body.$.id}}

### 10. Verify Ethan's Registration status
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/registrations/{{demoCreateRegistration2.response.body.$.id}}

### 11. Verify Maya's Registration status
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/registrations/{{demoCreateRegistration3.response.body.$.id}}